WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:06.529
Every country is famous for creating a certain
culinary concoction.

00:00:06.529 --> 00:00:11.500
Whatever you eat you can guarantee there’s
a story behind it and a certain country attached

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:12.710
to its creation.

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:18.101
But you shouldn’t always rely on these commonly
told countries of origin because some famous

00:00:18.101 --> 00:00:20.660
foods don’t actually come from where you
thought.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:30.260
Here are nine extremely common foods with
unexpected and surprising countries of origin.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:36.839
You’ve just had an enjoyable meal at a Chinese
restaurant, what would be the perfect thing

00:00:36.839 --> 00:00:38.110
to end your meal.

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:39.549
A scoop of ice cream?

00:00:39.549 --> 00:00:40.930
A coffee, perhaps?

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:44.729
No don’t be silly, you need to know your
fortune of course!

00:00:44.729 --> 00:00:48.960
Yes, you must find out if you’re going to
come into considerable wealth or if your partner

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.199
secretly hates your guts and desires to leave
you.

00:00:52.199 --> 00:00:56.820
Fortune cookies are popular in most western
countries, and foolishly most western countries

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:59.530
believe they’re actually Chinese.

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:04.280
Whereas in China they’re actually well known
for being an American invention.

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.990
But here’s the twist, you’re all wrong!

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:11.540
It’s true they showed up in the US well
before they were a thing in China.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:17.440
They were brought to California by the Japanese,
yes Japan!

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.090
Their origin dates back to the 19th century
in Kyoto, Japan.

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:25.560
The Japanese version of the cookie is called
“tsujiura senbei” and they are slightly

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:28.160
different from the ones we know today.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.590
They are a little bit bigger, darker in colour
and their batter contains sesame and miso,

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:34.680
instead of vanilla and butter.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:39.280
The cookies do contain a fortune, but the
small piece of paper is wedged into the bend

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.280
of the cookie, rather than placed inside.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:45.979
The cookies were modified to resemble the
ones we know today after they were brought

00:01:45.979 --> 00:01:47.320
to America.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:51.950
The original variation of the cookies are
still sold today, in some regions of Japan.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:56.210
Next up is the supposed Indian balti dish,
Chicken Tikka Masala.

00:01:56.210 --> 00:02:01.409
Whilst you would be forgiven for thinking
that this curry originated in India, it is

00:02:01.409 --> 00:02:05.770
actually believed to have been originally
invented in the United Kingdom.

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:09.119
Glasgow, Scotland, to be precise.

00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:13.870
The story goes that in the early 1970s, a
customer dining at the Indian restaurant Shish

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:19.420
Mahal in Glasgow complained about the dryness
of the chicken dish he was served and sent

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:21.150
it back to the kitchen.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:26.170
The chef, Ali Ahmed Aslam, quickly prepared
a sauce which included various spices and

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:31.400
a tin of Campbell’s condensed tomato soup
and the famous dish was born.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.160
The dish is completely unheard of in India.

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:38.260
When you think of France you probably think
of the Eiffel Tower and Beret’s.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:43.070
And just to throw some more fuel onto the
stereotypical fire, sitting outside a café,

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:48.100
smoking, drinking coffee and eating a croissant,
but it turns out that this classic French

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:51.459
breakfast pastry isn’t actually French.

00:02:51.459 --> 00:02:53.720
I know, it rather shocking.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:58.130
Going back as far as the 13th century, an
early ancestor of the croissant can be found

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:03.250
in the form of a traditional Austrian yeast
roll, which was made into crescent shapes

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:05.220
called a “Kipferl”.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:10.000
Although these types of pastries were found
throughout Europe, they were primarily Austrian.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:16.030
Austrian artillery officer August Zang brought
these to France when he founded his Viennese

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:22.250
bakery in Paris called “Boulangerie Viennoise”
between 1938 and 1939.

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:28.250
Kipferl’s were a hit with the Parisians,
so they nicked it, modified it a tiny bit

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:31.340
and the French version of the Austrian pastry
was born.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:34.360
They named it croissant after it’s cresent
shape.

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:38.290
Speaking of things that aren’t French: French
Toast.

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:43.430
In fact, there’s evidence to suggest that
the recipe of fried eggy bread was around

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:46.250
long before France was even a country.

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:50.730
In 4th century Rome, instead of discarding
their stale bread, they would try to make

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:54.400
it more edible by soaking it in a mixture
of egg and milk.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:04:00.530
This origin is still seen today in what we
know as French Toast, the French call it “Pain

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:03.629
Perdu”, which literally means “Lost Bread”.

00:04:03.629 --> 00:04:06.760
But how did it get the name “French Toast?”

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:13.890
Whilst this topic is widely debated, a popular
myth is that in 1724, an innkeeper named Joseph

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:19.699
French in Albany, New York, created the dish
and advertised it as “French Toast”.

00:04:19.699 --> 00:04:24.740
He meant to advertise it as “French’s
Toast” but the silly bugger forgot the apostrophe

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:25.949
“s” at the end.

00:04:25.949 --> 00:04:27.809
Next up is the Caesar Salad.

00:04:27.809 --> 00:04:32.180
Whilst it may be easy to assume that this
came from Rome and was probably named after

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:37.520
Julius Caesar, it may surprise you to learn
that this popular salad was actually invented

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:38.520
in Mexico.

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:44.559
The salad’s creator is widely recognised
as restaurateur Caesar Cardini, who is an

00:04:44.559 --> 00:04:49.419
Italian immigrant that operated restaurants
in Mexico where he created the dish on an

00:04:49.419 --> 00:04:55.190
alleged Fourth of July in 1924 when he ran
out of ingredients and had to make do with

00:04:55.190 --> 00:04:59.389
what he had, adding a bit of flair by table-side
tossing.

00:04:59.389 --> 00:05:01.060
On the subject of Mexico….

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:04.590
Fajitas aren’t Mexican, they’re Texan.

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:10.879
Just across the border in Texas in the 1940’s,
Mexican ranch workers living in Texas were

00:05:10.879 --> 00:05:16.449
given the least appetising parts of animals,
which were later butchered for food.

00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:21.020
Such as the head, intestines and a part which
came to be known as the “fajita” which

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:23.839
was a thin layer of muscle that covered the
diaphragm.

00:05:23.839 --> 00:05:29.509
The workers tenderised the meat by pounding
it, marinating it in lime juice and cooked

00:05:29.509 --> 00:05:34.039
it on an open fire before serving it rolled
up in a flour tortilla.

00:05:34.039 --> 00:05:37.979
The word “faja” is the Spanish word for
“strip”.

00:05:37.979 --> 00:05:42.639
The term “fajita” has developed over the
years from meaning a cheap piece of meat to

00:05:42.639 --> 00:05:48.469
describing pieces of meat wrapped in a tortilla,
but the word “fajita” according the Oxford

00:05:48.469 --> 00:05:52.380
English Dictionary did not appear in print
until 1971.

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:58.159
“Ninfa’s Restaurant” in Houston, Texas
is said to be the biggest development in what

00:05:58.159 --> 00:06:00.430
we today know today as “Fajitas”.

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:06.849
In 1973, an item appeared on the menu called
“Tacos al Carbon”, consisting of grilled

00:06:06.849 --> 00:06:10.330
meat strips, condiments and flour tortillas.

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:14.020
They later changed the name to “Fajitas’
in 1981.

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:18.240
Next up is the surprising origin of chips,
or French fries.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:22.771
They’re thought to have been invented in
17th century Belgium, although there is some

00:06:22.771 --> 00:06:26.629
debate going on about this as France claims
to have invented them first.

00:06:26.629 --> 00:06:28.439
But they totally didn’t.

00:06:28.439 --> 00:06:34.149
The tale goes that the people of Namur, Andenne
and Dinant in Belgium used to fry the little

00:06:34.149 --> 00:06:36.909
fish that they caught from the Meuse River.

00:06:36.909 --> 00:06:41.999
When the river froze over during harsh winters,
they would instead cut potatoes into the shape

00:06:41.999 --> 00:06:44.639
of little fish and fry those instead.

00:06:44.639 --> 00:06:49.620
As to the confusion of why they are called
“French Fries”, it is said that the Belgian

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:55.399
national dish “les frites” was simply
just given the wrong name by American servicemen

00:06:55.399 --> 00:06:58.719
at the end of the First World War when they
arrived in Belgium.

00:06:58.719 --> 00:07:03.439
They named the fried potatoes after the French
language spoken in Wallonia, the predominately

00:07:03.439 --> 00:07:09.210
French-speaking region of Belgium, therefore
translating “Les Frites” into “French

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:10.210
Fries”.

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:14.889
If you think that Spaghetti and Meatballs
is a traditional Italian dish, you’d be

00:07:14.889 --> 00:07:15.889
wrong.

00:07:15.889 --> 00:07:19.819
In fact, if you travel to Italy, it would
be very difficult to find Spaghetti and Meatballs

00:07:19.819 --> 00:07:24.800
on the menu, and if you do happen to find
it, it’s probably only being served to satisfy

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:26.229
the tourists.

00:07:26.229 --> 00:07:30.330
It turns out that Spaghetti and Meatballs
as we know it today was actually invented

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:33.319
in the USA by Italian immigrants.

00:07:33.319 --> 00:07:39.370
Between 1880 to 1920 around 4 million Italians
immigrated to America, and went from spending

00:07:39.370 --> 00:07:43.929
75% of their income on food to just 25%.

00:07:43.929 --> 00:07:48.029
Whereas before they had to make do with the
ingredients they could find and afford, they

00:07:48.029 --> 00:07:53.319
were now able to put meat on the table quite
easily, such as meatballs.

00:07:53.319 --> 00:07:58.620
With canned tomatoes being an easily accessible
source of food at most local stores, The Italian

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:04.469
marinara sauce, otherwise known as “Sailor
Sauce” governed Italian-American food.

00:08:04.469 --> 00:08:09.430
Combing these ingredients together with one
of the only Italian ingredients available,

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:14.779
spaghetti, the dish of Spaghetti and Meatballs
was born and made its way on to dinner tables

00:08:14.779 --> 00:08:16.009
all over the world.

00:08:16.009 --> 00:08:20.520
It was consequently given the false image
of an authentic Italian meal.

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:27.080
Last but certainly not least is the famous
flavoured icy treat that is ice cream.

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:32.240
This sumptuous dessert that we know and love
today is said to have been created in 17th

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:36.870
Century Italy but the reality is that ice
cream in some shape or form actually dates

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:40.440
back as far as 3,000 BC in China.

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:44.960
The ice cream back then however was a far
cry from the pre-packaged cornettos and magnums

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:46.620
that we enjoy today.

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:51.370
The Chinese would use a mixture of milk and
rice to make their iced cream.

00:08:51.370 --> 00:08:55.800
But in 3,000 BC, I’m fairly certain they
didn’t have freezers.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:59.720
So how did they make their ice cream, ice
cold?

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:04.740
They accomplished this by pouring a mixture
of snow and saltpetre around containers filled

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:06.520
with their ingredients.

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:11.020
Just how salt raises the boiling point of
water, it also lowers the freezing point to

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:12.020
below zero.

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:14.580
And there you have it, ice cream.

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:20.750
Fast forward to 1533, and Italian duchess
Catherine de’ Medici is credited for bringing

00:09:20.750 --> 00:09:22.620
ice cream to Europe.

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:27.660
She is said to have brought to France with
her some Italian chefs who had knowledge and

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:30.650
recipes for flavoured sorbets and ices.

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:36.870
100 years later a legend tells the tale of
how Charles I of England was so taken with

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:43.570
the “frozen snow” that he gave his personal
ice cream maker a lifetime pension in return

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:50.860
for keeping the formula a secret, so that
ice cream could be a royal prerogative.

